From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Johannes Stein <johannesstein@freeze-dev.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 11:49:18 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 70/77] wasm32: encode positive reference offsets as the memarg
 offset of loads and stores

prepare_stack_for_ref computed every address with a base register as
"local.get base; i32.const offset; i32.add" and then loaded or stored
with memarg offset 0. Engines see the sum as an ordinary i32 value:
Cranelift (wasmtime) hoists base+offset out of loops when the base is
loop-invariant (the frame pointer, Self) and, in large routines such as
an interpreter's dispatch loop, keeps the hoisted sums in spill slots,
so each access became a reload of the address from the native stack
plus the load. A positive offset now stays in the reference and is
written as the memarg offset of the load/store that follows, which the
engine folds into the addressing mode (x86: one mov off(heap,base)).

The memarg offset is added without wrapping (an access whose base +
offset exceeds 4 GiB traps), i32.add wraps. That only differs if the
base register is not an address but an address minus a constant. The
generic indexed array access produces exactly that: it leaves the array
offset, minus the lower bound and plus a constant added to the index
("a[i+5]"), times the element size, in the reference's offset, so
base+index can be below address 0 for valid accesses (an array
[-1000000..0] at a low address). twasmvecnode therefore adds such a
positive offset to the address right after the index; offsets added
later (fields, the parts of an inline record copy) are relative to the
element itself. Negative offsets keep the explicit i32.sub.

Test: tests/test/cg/tarrlow1.pp (negative lower bounds, index plus a
constant, record copies of array elements; passes on x86_64 and wasm32).

GocciaScript VM under wasmtime 49 (callgrind instruction counts): AWFY
geomean 0.949x (all 17 workloads 0.914x..0.979x); GocciaRunner.wasm
30.57 MB -> 29.24 MB.
